description.narrative:
Some examples of activities during 2024:

Egypt 

• Research (December 2024 - February 2025) enabled to identify potential companies in the field of recycling for matchmaking and collaboration with Swiss companies under the initiative Sustainable Textiles Switzerland (STS2030);
• Following the project agreement signature, the call for expression of interest was launched on 5th January 2025 enabling the selection process of beneficiaries to start.
• 3 studies published on the impact of the textile & clothing (T&C) sector on (i) climate change, (ii) poverty reduction, (iii) the role of women in the sector;
• 5 Business Support Organizations (BSOs) and academic institutions enhanced their knowledge of the twin transition following ITCs study tour of textiles innovations in Sweden (25-28 November 2024);
• 1 restitution workshop attended by 26 industry experts enabled to present the results of ITCs study tour of textiles innovations in Sweden.

Jordan

• 18/23 beneficiary SMEs (incl. designers, SMEs, locomotive companies) gained increased understanding of digital transformation concepts through their participation in Smart Factory training;
• 16/23 SMEs received awareness-raising sessions on lean manufacturing, followed by 10 field visits and coaching sessions to introduce change and improve efficiency.
• 11 SMEs were represented at the MAGIC Show Trade Fair to support international markets access;
• 5 BSOs enhanced their knowledge of sustainability and circularity innovations through ITCs study tour of textiles innovations in Sweden

Morocco

• Inbound mission of 11 European companies (5 Portuguese, 6 Spanish) at the Maroc in Mode fair in Casablanca (7-8 November 2024);
• 5 BSOs roadmaps were updated with key recommendations linked to T&C-related sustainable trade and circularity;
• The Moroccan institutional visit to Switzerland led to visits of innovative companies, R&D centres, and incubators, and to the signature of an MoU between Swiss Textile and AMITH;
• 5 BSOs enhanced their knowledge of sustainability and circularity innovations through ITCs study tour of textiles innovations in Sweden.

Tunisia

• 5 beneficiary SMEs obtained environmental and social certifications in 2024 (including 2 new certifications and 3 renewals);
• 18 SMEs managers and 8 fashion designers integrated key sustainability principles in the T&C sector due to a better understanding;
• 17 SMEs and 7 fashion designers enhanced their market access and sustainability performance by acquiring the necessary knowledge and skills to comply with relevant social and environmental standards and certifications in the textile sector;
• 6 BSOs enhanced their knowledge of sustainability and circularity innovations through ITCs study tour of textiles innovations in Sweden.

Results:

Across the 128 beneficiary companies, export performance in 2024 largely reflected sector-level trends per country. In Jordan, beneficiary companies saw a 6% export increase, in line with the sectors 7% growth. Tunisia and Morocco experienced declines in exports, with beneficiary companies reporting a 6% and 10% drop, respectively. This aligned with broader sector trends, where exports fell by 5% in Tunisia and grew only marginally (2%) in Morocco. Overall, total sales and exports of beneficiary companies saw a drop of 26.5 million USD and 20.8 million USD respectively in the four countries. Key factors behind these declines included the loss of major clients, and saturation in key markets such as Spain and France. For this reason, for example, nine market linkages were facilitated especially between Moroccan companies and Portuguese/Spanish buyers, with potential contracts in the pipeline.

Despite the overall export decline, total employment across the four countries increased by 288 jobs, with the largest gains in Sri Lanka (164) and Tunisia (86), while Jordan (35) and Morocco (3) saw more modest growth. In Sri Lanka and Jordan, job creation followed the export increases. In Tunisia and Morocco, whereas exports fell by 5.16 million USD and 18.9 million USD, respectively, employment still grew slightly. However, the increase remains small relative to the total workforce in both countries but indicating that companies are retaining employees likely with the hope of a recovery in 2025.
